1
01:00:00,033 --> 01:00:04,233
<b>On August 8th, 1947, a draft map was sent</b>

2
01:00:04,233 --> 01:00:06,699
<b>from the Viceroy's office in New Delhi to</b>

3
01:00:06,699 --> 01:00:07,800
<b>the governor of Punjab.</b>

4
01:00:08,866 --> 01:00:10,033
<b>It showed the new border</b>

5
01:00:10,033 --> 01:00:11,566
<b>between India and Pakistan.</b>

6
01:00:12,033 --> 01:00:16,099
<b>Ferrozepur, a district that was 55%</b>

7
01:00:16,566 --> 01:00:18,966
<b>Muslim, containing the kennel head works</b>

8
01:00:18,966 --> 01:00:20,933
<b>that controlled irrigation for millions</b>

9
01:00:20,933 --> 01:00:22,266
<b>of acres of Pakistani</b>

10
01:00:22,266 --> 01:00:24,966
<b>farmland, was on the Pakistani side.</b>

11
01:00:25,766 --> 01:00:27,233
<b>Three days later, a</b>

12
01:00:27,233 --> 01:00:28,766
<b>single coded message arrived.</b>

13
01:00:29,866 --> 01:00:30,599
<b>Two words.</b>

14
01:00:31,599 --> 01:00:32,966
<b>Eliminate salient.</b>

15
01:00:33,733 --> 01:00:35,533
<b>Ferrozepur was now Indian.</b>

16
01:00:36,333 --> 01:00:38,466
<b>And with it, the valve controlling</b>

17
01:00:38,466 --> 01:00:39,966
<b>Pakistan's water supply.</b>

18
01:00:40,900 --> 01:00:42,466
<b>No census data changed.</b>

19
01:00:43,033 --> 01:00:44,433
<b>No infrastructure moved.</b>

20
01:00:45,133 --> 01:00:46,866
<b>No new evidence was submitted.</b>

21
01:00:47,900 --> 01:00:49,233
<b>The man who drew the border,</b>

22
01:00:50,033 --> 01:00:52,333
<b>a British chancery lawyer who had never</b>

23
01:00:52,333 --> 01:00:53,433
<b>been east of Gibraltar,</b>

24
01:00:54,333 --> 01:00:56,366
<b>who was simultaneously drawing a second</b>

25
01:00:56,366 --> 01:00:59,233
<b>border for Bengal from a desk in Simla,</b>

26
01:00:59,833 --> 01:01:01,166
<b>using maps he later</b>

27
01:01:01,166 --> 01:01:02,433
<b>admitted were incorrect,</b>

28
01:01:03,300 --> 01:01:04,366
<b>burned every working</b>

29
01:01:04,366 --> 01:01:06,166
<b>paper and left India forever.</b>

30
01:01:07,633 --> 01:01:09,766
<b>So, I read the actual documents.</b>

31
01:01:10,233 --> 01:01:11,199
<b>The Radcliffe Award.</b>

32
01:01:11,766 --> 01:01:13,533
<b>The Boundary Commission proceedings.</b>

33
01:01:14,300 --> 01:01:15,900
<b>The transfer of power files.</b>

34
01:01:16,566 --> 01:01:18,666
<b>And what they show is a three-day window</b>

35
01:01:18,666 --> 01:01:20,966
<b>where the most consequential border in</b>

36
01:01:20,966 --> 01:01:23,166
<b>modern South Asian history was redrawn.</b>

37
01:01:24,266 --> 01:01:25,699
<b>And nobody can prove why.</b>

38
01:01:26,533 --> 01:01:28,300
<b>Here's who they choose to draw the line.</b>

39
01:01:29,266 --> 01:01:30,133
<b>Cyril Radcliffe.</b>

40
01:01:30,866 --> 01:01:32,166
<b>48 years old.</b>

41
01:01:32,633 --> 01:01:33,533
<b>A chancery lawyer.</b>

42
01:01:33,966 --> 01:01:35,233
<b>His career was trusts,</b>

43
01:01:35,666 --> 01:01:37,400
<b>estates, corporate structures.</b>

44
01:01:38,199 --> 01:01:40,366
<b>His wartime job was Director General of</b>

45
01:01:40,366 --> 01:01:41,666
<b>the Ministry of Information.</b>

46
01:01:42,366 --> 01:01:43,233
<b>Government censorship.</b>

47
01:01:44,133 --> 01:01:46,333
<b>It never educated the border dispute.</b>

48
01:01:47,033 --> 01:01:48,366
<b>It never studied demographics.</b>

49
01:01:49,266 --> 01:01:50,966
<b>It never even set foot in Asia.</b>

50
01:01:52,433 --> 01:01:53,233
<b>According to Stanley</b>

51
01:01:53,233 --> 01:01:55,533
<b>Wolpert in Jinnah of Pakistan,</b>

52
01:01:56,800 --> 01:01:58,400
<b>Radcliffe, who had never even visited</b>

53
01:01:58,400 --> 01:02:00,066
<b>India and expressed no</b>

54
01:02:00,066 --> 01:02:01,766
<b>known opinions on its problems,</b>

55
01:02:02,533 --> 01:02:04,633
<b>was unanimously accepted and would soon</b>

56
01:02:04,633 --> 01:02:06,300
<b>decide the destiny of millions</b>

57
01:02:06,300 --> 01:02:08,733
<b>of Hindus, Sikhs and Muslims.</b>

58
01:02:09,666 --> 01:02:10,833
<b>That was the qualification.</b>

59
01:02:12,199 --> 01:02:12,900
<b>Total ignorance.</b>

60
01:02:14,066 --> 01:02:14,766
<b>A man with no</b>

61
01:02:14,766 --> 01:02:18,033
<b>attachments, no opinions, no stake.</b>

62
01:02:18,666 --> 01:02:19,566
<b>Supposedly impartial.</b>

63
01:02:19,866 --> 01:02:22,433
<b>Precisely because he knew nothing.</b>

64
01:02:23,733 --> 01:02:25,533
<b>He arrived on July 8th.</b>

65
01:02:25,533 --> 01:02:26,966
<b>He had until mid-August.</b>

66
01:02:28,000 --> 01:02:29,866
<b>36 days to draw a border</b>

67
01:02:29,866 --> 01:02:31,666
<b>for 400 million people.</b>

68
01:02:32,699 --> 01:02:33,400
<b>And here's the part</b>

69
01:02:33,400 --> 01:02:34,633
<b>that rarely gets mentioned.</b>

70
01:02:35,133 --> 01:02:37,133
<b>He wasn't drawing one border.</b>

71
01:02:37,866 --> 01:02:39,533
<b>He was chairing two boundary</b>

72
01:02:39,533 --> 01:02:41,133
<b>commissions simultaneously.</b>

73
01:02:42,266 --> 01:02:42,833
<b>Punjab and Bengal.</b>

74
01:02:43,833 --> 01:02:45,866
<b>He couldn't physically attend the Punjab</b>

75
01:02:45,866 --> 01:02:47,500
<b>public hearings in Lahore</b>

76
01:02:47,500 --> 01:02:49,300
<b>because he was processing Bengal</b>

77
01:02:49,300 --> 01:02:50,633
<b>paperwork from Delhi.</b>

78
01:02:51,733 --> 01:02:53,266
<b>As Yasmin Khan writes,</b>

79
01:02:53,633 --> 01:02:56,333
<b>"The border was devised from a distance.</b>

80
01:02:56,833 --> 01:02:58,333
<b>The land, villages and</b>

81
01:02:58,333 --> 01:02:59,633
<b>communities to be divided</b>

82
01:02:59,633 --> 01:03:02,166
<b>were not visited or inspected by the</b>

83
01:03:02,166 --> 01:03:03,533
<b>imperial map maker."</b>

84
01:03:04,300 --> 01:03:05,333
<b>He later told the</b>

85
01:03:05,333 --> 01:03:06,699
<b>journalist Kulledip Nair.</b>

86
01:03:07,633 --> 01:03:10,199
<b>Even district maps were not available and</b>

87
01:03:10,199 --> 01:03:11,833
<b>the ones there were were incorrect.</b>

88
01:03:11,833 --> 01:03:14,766
<b>His official instructions, the terms of</b>

89
01:03:14,766 --> 01:03:16,500
<b>reference, said this.</b>

90
01:03:16,833 --> 01:03:18,766
<b>"The boundary commission is instructed to</b>

91
01:03:18,766 --> 01:03:19,933
<b>demarcate the boundaries</b>

92
01:03:19,933 --> 01:03:21,733
<b>on the basis of ascertaining the</b>

93
01:03:21,733 --> 01:03:23,266
<b>contiguous majority areas</b>

94
01:03:23,266 --> 01:03:24,866
<b>of Muslims and non-Muslims.</b>

95
01:03:25,699 --> 01:03:27,933
<b>In doing so, it will also take into</b>

96
01:03:27,933 --> 01:03:29,866
<b>account other factors."</b>

97
01:03:30,866 --> 01:03:33,333
<b>Other factors? Never defined.</b>

98
01:03:33,800 --> 01:03:36,833
<b>It could mean railways, canals, economic</b>

99
01:03:36,833 --> 01:03:38,466
<b>viability, anything.</b>

100
01:03:39,333 --> 01:03:42,699
<b>Nehru was asked to clarify. He refused.</b>

101
01:03:43,800 --> 01:03:45,766
<b>So the mandate was, "Follow the</b>

102
01:03:45,766 --> 01:03:49,199
<b>demographics unless you decide not to."</b>

103
01:03:49,466 --> 01:03:50,633
<b>And the demographic data</b>

104
01:03:50,633 --> 01:03:52,566
<b>was from a 1941 census.</b>

105
01:03:53,199 --> 01:03:55,566
<b>Six years out of date, Radcliffe chaired</b>

106
01:03:55,566 --> 01:03:56,866
<b>a four-member commission.</b>

107
01:03:57,533 --> 01:03:58,566
<b>Two judges nominated</b>

108
01:03:58,566 --> 01:03:59,633
<b>by the Muslim League.</b>

109
01:04:00,133 --> 01:04:01,866
<b>Two by Congress and the Sikhs.</b>

110
01:04:02,500 --> 01:04:04,566
<b>They deadlocked on every issue.</b>

111
01:04:05,033 --> 01:04:06,033
<b>Two against two.</b>

112
01:04:06,866 --> 01:04:07,500
<b>So the Indian</b>

113
01:04:07,500 --> 01:04:10,199
<b>Independence Act was amended so that</b>

114
01:04:10,433 --> 01:04:11,766
<b>"Award meant the</b>

115
01:04:11,766 --> 01:04:13,166
<b>decision of the chairman alone.</b>

116
01:04:13,933 --> 01:04:15,500
<b>One man making all the calls."</b>

117
01:04:16,433 --> 01:04:18,699
<b>Patrick French, in Liberty or Death,</b>

118
01:04:19,066 --> 01:04:21,633
<b>documents what happened on August 8th.</b>

119
01:04:21,833 --> 01:04:25,166
<b>On 8th August, George Abel sent a rough</b>

120
01:04:25,166 --> 01:04:26,266
<b>map featuring the</b>

121
01:04:26,266 --> 01:04:27,433
<b>pharaohs of poor Salient</b>

122
01:04:27,433 --> 01:04:28,566
<b>to the private office</b>

123
01:04:28,566 --> 01:04:29,966
<b>officer, Avon Jenkins.</b>

124
01:04:30,866 --> 01:04:32,833
<b>An accompanying letter by Abel said,</b>

125
01:04:32,833 --> 01:04:34,833
<b>"There will not be any great</b>

126
01:04:34,833 --> 01:04:36,466
<b>changes from this boundary."</b>

127
01:04:37,033 --> 01:04:37,833
<b>Abel was Lord</b>

128
01:04:37,833 --> 01:04:39,733
<b>Mombatin's private secretary.</b>

129
01:04:40,533 --> 01:04:42,033
<b>Jenkins was the governor of Punjab.</b>

130
01:04:43,099 --> 01:04:45,066
<b>He'd been begging for advance information</b>

131
01:04:45,066 --> 01:04:47,266
<b>so he could deploy police before the</b>

132
01:04:47,266 --> 01:04:48,333
<b>border was announced.</b>

133
01:04:48,733 --> 01:04:50,199
<b>Here's why the August 8th</b>

134
01:04:50,199 --> 01:04:52,133
<b>map made geographic sense.</b>

135
01:04:52,733 --> 01:04:55,900
<b>Fursopur Teghsil was 55% Muslim.</b>

136
01:04:56,366 --> 01:04:58,833
<b>Zira Teghsil was 65% Muslim.</b>

137
01:04:59,199 --> 01:05:00,233
<b>The demographic mandate</b>

138
01:05:00,233 --> 01:05:01,833
<b>pointed clearly to Pakistan.</b>

139
01:05:01,866 --> 01:05:05,166
<b>But these Teghsils weren't just farmland.</b>

140
01:05:05,833 --> 01:05:07,400
<b>They contained the headworks of the</b>

141
01:05:07,400 --> 01:05:09,333
<b>Sutlek Valley Canal system.</b>

142
01:05:09,833 --> 01:05:11,500
<b>The physical infrastructure that</b>

143
01:05:11,500 --> 01:05:13,333
<b>controlled water flow to millions of</b>

144
01:05:13,333 --> 01:05:15,366
<b>acres of irrigated land downstream.</b>

145
01:05:16,400 --> 01:05:17,933
<b>Land that, after</b>

146
01:05:17,933 --> 01:05:19,766
<b>partition, would be in Pakistan.</b>

147
01:05:20,866 --> 01:05:22,033
<b>Think of it as a valve.</b>

148
01:05:22,800 --> 01:05:25,466
<b>Whoever held fursopur held a tap.</b>

149
01:05:26,666 --> 01:05:29,233
<b>And the August 8th draft gave the tap to</b>

150
01:05:29,233 --> 01:05:30,833
<b>the same country that needed the water.</b>

151
01:05:30,866 --> 01:05:33,466
<b>The letter said, "There</b>

152
01:05:33,466 --> 01:05:34,866
<b>will not be any great changes."</b>

153
01:05:36,033 --> 01:05:37,933
<b>Then something changed.</b>

154
01:05:38,599 --> 01:05:41,000
<b>Two or three days later, Jenkins received</b>

155
01:05:41,000 --> 01:05:42,366
<b>a coded telephone message.</b>

156
01:05:42,733 --> 01:05:43,666
<b>A sacriphone call</b>

157
01:05:43,666 --> 01:05:45,033
<b>from the Viceroy's house.</b>

158
01:05:45,333 --> 01:05:46,000
<b>French again.</b>

159
01:05:46,733 --> 01:05:48,933
<b>Jenkins received a sacriphone message</b>

160
01:05:48,933 --> 01:05:51,466
<b>telling him to eliminate salient.</b>

161
01:05:52,266 --> 01:05:53,833
<b>Meaning that the whole of Fursopur</b>

162
01:05:53,833 --> 01:05:55,833
<b>District would now remain in India.</b>

163
01:05:55,866 --> 01:05:58,733
<b>48,000 Muslim residents</b>

164
01:05:58,733 --> 01:06:00,666
<b>according to the 1941 census.</b>

165
01:06:01,133 --> 01:06:02,666
<b>This now became Indian.</b>

166
01:06:03,266 --> 01:06:04,433
<b>A race from Pakistan's</b>

167
01:06:04,433 --> 01:06:06,000
<b>side with a single phone call.</b>

168
01:06:06,800 --> 01:06:09,066
<b>And with them, the canal headworks.</b>

169
01:06:09,800 --> 01:06:10,666
<b>The valve shut.</b>

170
01:06:11,366 --> 01:06:13,733
<b>Within months, India would unilaterally</b>

171
01:06:13,733 --> 01:06:16,133
<b>cut off water to Pakistani canals.</b>

172
01:06:16,699 --> 01:06:18,566
<b>Triggering an irrigation crisis that took</b>

173
01:06:18,566 --> 01:06:19,866
<b>12 years to resolve.</b>

174
01:06:21,033 --> 01:06:22,699
<b>So, what happened between</b>

175
01:06:22,699 --> 01:06:24,533
<b>August 8th and August 10th?</b>

176
01:06:25,133 --> 01:06:28,099
<b>On August 9th, Radcliffe met Lord Ismay,</b>

177
01:06:28,566 --> 01:06:30,833
<b>Mountbatten's chief of staff, for an</b>

178
01:06:30,833 --> 01:06:32,833
<b>evening drink at the Viceroy's estate.</b>

179
01:06:33,433 --> 01:06:34,066
<b>The meeting is</b>

180
01:06:34,066 --> 01:06:36,566
<b>documented, but as Khan writes,</b>

181
01:06:37,033 --> 01:06:39,166
<b>"There is no contemporary record of it,</b>

182
01:06:39,166 --> 01:06:41,199
<b>and the recollections of the three</b>

183
01:06:41,199 --> 01:06:42,266
<b>participants differ."</b>

184
01:06:43,166 --> 01:06:45,500
<b>Years later, Christopher Beaumont,</b>

185
01:06:46,033 --> 01:06:47,233
<b>Radcliffe's own private</b>

186
01:06:47,233 --> 01:06:48,833
<b>secretary, gave testimony.</b>

187
01:06:49,833 --> 01:06:52,033
<b>In 1989, and again in</b>

188
01:06:52,033 --> 01:06:54,833
<b>1992, Beaumont stated that,</b>

189
01:06:55,233 --> 01:06:56,566
<b>"M Mountbatten, allegedly</b>

190
01:06:56,566 --> 01:06:58,066
<b>under pressure from Nehru,</b>

191
01:06:58,666 --> 01:07:00,266
<b>persuaded Radcliffe to change the</b>

192
01:07:00,266 --> 01:07:02,233
<b>Punjab's borderline in India's favour."</b>

193
01:07:03,366 --> 01:07:04,366
<b>And there's more.</b>

194
01:07:05,066 --> 01:07:07,266
<b>John Christie, Mountbatten's joint</b>

195
01:07:07,266 --> 01:07:09,466
<b>private secretary, kept a diary.</b>

196
01:07:10,333 --> 01:07:12,466
<b>His entry for August 9th says,</b>

197
01:07:12,833 --> 01:07:15,800
<b>Mountbatten was in a tired flap and had</b>

198
01:07:15,800 --> 01:07:18,400
<b>to be strenuously dissuaded from asking</b>

199
01:07:18,400 --> 01:07:20,066
<b>Radcliffe to alter his award.</b>

200
01:07:20,733 --> 01:07:22,000
<b>Strenuously dissuaded.</b>

201
01:07:22,733 --> 01:07:24,000
<b>Meaning, someone had</b>

202
01:07:24,000 --> 01:07:25,133
<b>to talk him out of it.</b>

203
01:07:25,400 --> 01:07:28,199
<b>Which suggests he was trying to do it.</b>

204
01:07:28,866 --> 01:07:29,733
<b>If we're being fair,</b>

205
01:07:30,066 --> 01:07:31,033
<b>the evidence against...</b>

206
01:07:31,833 --> 01:07:35,133
<b>If we're being fair, the evidence against</b>

207
01:07:35,133 --> 01:07:36,233
<b>intervention is real.</b>

208
01:07:36,966 --> 01:07:37,866
<b>Mountbatten maintained</b>

209
01:07:37,866 --> 01:07:39,766
<b>that he and Radcliffe were,</b>

210
01:07:40,066 --> 01:07:42,733
<b>"mutually most careful never to have any</b>

211
01:07:42,733 --> 01:07:44,266
<b>discussions about the boundaries."</b>

212
01:07:45,133 --> 01:07:47,466
<b>Radcliffe himself called his award the</b>

213
01:07:47,466 --> 01:07:49,733
<b>result of his own unfettered judgement.</b>

214
01:07:50,566 --> 01:07:53,133
<b>And the "other factors clause" does</b>

215
01:07:53,133 --> 01:07:54,599
<b>provide a legitimate rationale.</b>

216
01:07:55,699 --> 01:07:56,433
<b>Radcliffe wrote,</b>

217
01:07:57,099 --> 01:08:04,766
<b>I have come to the conclusion that there</b>

218
01:08:04,766 --> 01:08:06,833
<b>are factors such as the disruption of</b>

219
01:08:06,833 --> 01:08:07,466
<b>railway communications</b>

220
01:08:07,866 --> 01:08:10,966
<b>and water systems that ought in this</b>

221
01:08:10,966 --> 01:08:13,233
<b>instance to displace the primary claims</b>

222
01:08:13,233 --> 01:08:14,633
<b>of continuous majorities.</b>

223
01:08:15,666 --> 01:08:17,600
<b>Canalhead works, railway networks.</b>

224
01:08:18,500 --> 01:08:20,333
<b>These are real infrastructure concerns.</b>

225
01:08:21,166 --> 01:08:23,100
<b>So you could read these two ways.</b>

226
01:08:23,833 --> 01:08:25,133
<b>Either Mountbatten pressured</b>

227
01:08:25,133 --> 01:08:26,399
<b>Radcliffe to change the line</b>

228
01:08:26,833 --> 01:08:27,433
<b>and Radcliffe</b>

229
01:08:27,433 --> 01:08:29,933
<b>retrospectively justified it.</b>

230
01:08:29,933 --> 01:08:32,566
<b>Or Radcliffe genuinely reconsidered his</b>

231
01:08:32,566 --> 01:08:35,166
<b>draft and reached a different conclusion</b>

232
01:08:35,166 --> 01:08:36,366
<b>on legitimate grounds.</b>

233
01:08:36,866 --> 01:08:38,066
<b>We won't know for sure</b>

234
01:08:38,066 --> 01:08:39,833
<b>because of what happened next.</b>

235
01:08:40,233 --> 01:08:41,033
<b>Radcliffe signed the</b>

236
01:08:41,033 --> 01:08:43,066
<b>complete award on August 12th.</b>

237
01:08:43,066 --> 01:08:45,300
<b>It was published on August 17th.</b>

238
01:08:45,300 --> 01:08:47,366
<b>Two days after both India and Pakistan</b>

239
01:08:47,366 --> 01:08:48,800
<b>celebrated independence.</b>

240
01:08:49,833 --> 01:08:51,966
<b>People in borderland villages didn't know</b>

241
01:08:51,966 --> 01:08:53,433
<b>which country they lived in</b>

242
01:08:53,433 --> 01:08:55,133
<b>until two days after their</b>

243
01:08:55,133 --> 01:08:56,433
<b>countries already existed.</b>

244
01:08:57,433 --> 01:09:00,066
<b>And then Radcliffe destroyed everything.</b>

245
01:09:00,666 --> 01:09:02,333
<b>Alois Arthur Michael confirms.</b>

246
01:09:03,000 --> 01:09:05,933
<b>Sir Cyril's personal notes, memoranda and</b>

247
01:09:05,933 --> 01:09:08,100
<b>drafts were all destroyed at the time.</b>

248
01:09:08,899 --> 01:09:10,166
<b>Every working paper,</b>

249
01:09:10,633 --> 01:09:12,033
<b>every draft map gone.</b>

250
01:09:12,633 --> 01:09:13,966
<b>He refused his salary.</b>

251
01:09:14,300 --> 01:09:16,500
<b>He never returned to the subcontinent.</b>

252
01:09:17,133 --> 01:09:20,199
<b>Months before his death in 1977, someone</b>

253
01:09:20,199 --> 01:09:22,133
<b>asked if he'd ever go back.</b>

254
01:09:22,866 --> 01:09:23,600
<b>His answer?</b>

255
01:09:24,800 --> 01:09:27,133
<b>God forbid. Not even if they asked me.</b>

256
01:09:27,500 --> 01:09:29,333
<b>I suspect they'd shoot me out of hand.</b>

257
01:09:29,833 --> 01:09:31,466
<b>On both sides, Ferizapur</b>

258
01:09:31,466 --> 01:09:32,766
<b>gets the most attention.</b>

259
01:09:33,300 --> 01:09:33,766
<b>But the bigger</b>

260
01:09:33,766 --> 01:09:35,566
<b>consequence may be Godaspur.</b>

261
01:09:36,133 --> 01:09:39,033
<b>Godaspur contains the Papancot Road, the</b>

262
01:09:39,033 --> 01:09:41,533
<b>only land corridor from India to Kashmir.</b>

263
01:09:42,133 --> 01:09:43,033
<b>Michael is direct.</b>

264
01:09:43,633 --> 01:09:45,533
<b>Had Radcliffe avoided Godaspur to</b>

265
01:09:45,533 --> 01:09:47,666
<b>Pakistan, there would have been no land</b>

266
01:09:47,666 --> 01:09:48,766
<b>communication between</b>

267
01:09:48,766 --> 01:09:50,600
<b>India and Jammu-Kashmir.</b>

268
01:09:51,133 --> 01:09:53,766
<b>In 1965, 18 years after</b>

269
01:09:53,766 --> 01:09:55,833
<b>drawing the line, Radcliffe said,</b>

270
01:09:55,833 --> 01:09:58,533
<b>"I wouldn't dream of defending some of</b>

271
01:09:58,533 --> 01:09:59,366
<b>the decisions in</b>

272
01:09:59,366 --> 01:10:00,966
<b>detail I made at the time.</b>

273
01:10:01,333 --> 01:10:02,566
<b>I was not aware of the</b>

274
01:10:02,566 --> 01:10:03,633
<b>Kashmir thing at all."</b>

275
01:10:03,933 --> 01:10:04,766
<b>He says he wasn't</b>

276
01:10:04,766 --> 01:10:06,166
<b>even thinking of Kashmir.</b>

277
01:10:06,833 --> 01:10:08,466
<b>If that's true, the road</b>

278
01:10:08,466 --> 01:10:09,666
<b>corridor was an accident.</b>

279
01:10:10,233 --> 01:10:11,966
<b>If it's not, it was the most</b>

280
01:10:11,966 --> 01:10:13,899
<b>consequential geographic decision in</b>

281
01:10:13,899 --> 01:10:15,433
<b>modern South Asian history.</b>

282
01:10:15,800 --> 01:10:17,699
<b>The scholarly consensus is that this</b>

283
01:10:17,699 --> 01:10:19,000
<b>wasn't a grand conspiracy.</b>

284
01:10:19,566 --> 01:10:21,066
<b>It was a shoddy</b>

285
01:10:21,066 --> 01:10:23,566
<b>implementation of a wafer-thin plan.</b>

286
01:10:24,333 --> 01:10:26,533
<b>Exhaustion, haste, and a</b>

287
01:10:26,533 --> 01:10:27,933
<b>desire to escape liability.</b>

288
01:10:29,133 --> 01:10:30,366
<b>But here's what bothers me.</b>

289
01:10:30,800 --> 01:10:32,233
<b>We'll never know for certain what</b>

290
01:10:32,233 --> 01:10:34,699
<b>happened on August 9, 1947.</b>

291
01:10:35,466 --> 01:10:36,800
<b>Because the only man who could have</b>

292
01:10:36,800 --> 01:10:39,600
<b>settled it burned the evidence and took</b>

293
01:10:39,600 --> 01:10:40,766
<b>the answer to his grave.</b>

294
01:10:42,000 --> 01:10:43,066
<b>Those are the documents.</b>

295
01:10:43,733 --> 01:10:46,166
<b>What you conclude from them is up to you.</b>

296
01:10:46,766 --> 01:10:48,066
<b>Full sources in the description.</b>

297
01:10:48,966 --> 01:10:49,666
<b>Thank you for watching.</b>

